Files VeriTrace generates: CC0-1.0 OR MIT
=========================================

VeriTrace itself is MIT (see LICENSE). This file is about something else — the
files VeriTrace *writes into your project*:

  * SVA and Verilog checkers from `veritrace gen-sva`   (SPEC §8.22)
  * stimulus testbenches from `veritrace stimgen`       (SPEC §8.32)
  * formal harnesses, synthesis shims and any other source VeriTrace emits

Those files land next to your RTL, get committed to your repository, and may
end up compiled into your verification flow. So there must be no question about
what license they carry.

    SPDX-License-Identifier: CC0-1.0 OR MIT

To the extent possible under law, the authors of VeriTrace waive all copyright
and related or neighbouring rights to the output VeriTrace generates, under
CC0 1.0 Universal (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). Where
a public-domain dedication is not recognised or not convenient, the same files
are alternatively available under the MIT license in LICENSE, and you may pick
either at your option.

In plain terms: **the output is yours.** Keep it, edit it, ship it, relicense
it, and attribute nothing. VeriTrace's MIT terms — including the requirement to
carry a copyright notice — apply to VeriTrace's own source, not to what it
prints for you.

Why this is spelled out (SPEC §1.8)
-----------------------------------

VeriTrace reads RTL and waveforms as *data*. It does not link into your design
and nothing it computes ends up in a netlist, so the tool's license cannot
reach your code by that route. `gen-sva` is the one exception worth naming:
it emits source. An emitter with an unstated license invites the question of
whether its output "inherits" the tool's terms. It does not, and this file is
that answer in writing.

Every generated file carries the SPDX line above in its header, so the answer
travels with the file rather than living only here.
